 <b2210356065@cs.hacettepe.edu.tr>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 15:21:30 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 34/71] Default the packaged config to the Z.ai provider

An unset `model_provider` resolves to OpenAI, so the TUI patched the resolved
provider after building its startup `Config`. That fixed one object rather than
the rule: every thread transition that reloads config from disk lost it. Taking
"clear context and implement" from the plan prompt runs
`refresh_in_memory_config_from_disk_best_effort`, which replaced the patched
config and started the new thread on OpenAI, producing a 401 against
api.openai.com mid-session.

Move the default into the packaged defaults layer instead. It is compiled into
the binary at the lowest precedence, so every load path sees it -- TUI startup,
the app-server's own reloads, exec, doctor -- and a user's `config.toml` still
overrides it. In-process harnesses that build a `Config` without the loader keep
resolving to OpenAI, which is why the default was kept out of the loader itself.
---
 codex-rs/config/defaults.toml |  1 +
 codex-rs/tui/src/lib.rs       | 32 +-------------------------------
 2 files changed, 2 insertions(+), 31 deletions(-)

diff --git a/codex-rs/config/defaults.toml b/codex-rs/config/defaults.toml
index 42e3db5ba0..e91fdc52a4 100644
--- a/codex-rs/config/defaults.toml
+++ b/codex-rs/config/defaults.toml
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 # Fixed defaults for packaged Suffice clients.
+model_provider = "zai"
 include_permissions_instructions = true
 include_apps_instructions = true
 include_collaboration_mode_instructions = true
diff --git a/codex-rs/tui/src/lib.rs b/codex-rs/tui/src/lib.rs
index 6bf2900f7f..d1043145ed 100644
--- a/codex-rs/tui/src/lib.rs
+++ b/codex-rs/tui/src/lib.rs
@@ -55,7 +55,6 @@ use codex_login::default_client::originator;
 use codex_login::default_client::set_default_client_residency_requirement;
 use codex_login::enforce_login_restrictions;
 use codex_login::is_workload_identity_selected;
-use codex_model_provider_info::ZAI_PROVIDER_ID;
 use codex_protocol::ThreadId;
 use codex_protocol::auth::AuthMode;
 use codex_protocol::config_types::AltScreenMode;
@@ -1854,7 +1853,6 @@ async fn load_config_or_exit_with_fallback_cwd(
     strict_config: bool,
     fallback_cwd: Option<PathBuf>,
 ) -> Config {
-    let provider_overridden = overrides.model_provider.is_some();
     #[allow(clippy::print_stderr)]
     match ConfigBuilder::default()
         .cli_overrides(cli_kv_overrides)
@@ -1866,12 +1864,7 @@ async fn load_config_or_exit_with_fallback_cwd(
         .build()
         .await
     {
-        Ok(mut config) => {
-            if !provider_overridden {
-                apply_default_model_provider(&mut config);
-            }
-            config
-        }
+        Ok(config) => config,
         Err(err) => {
             restore_terminal_before_fatal_exit();
             eprintln!("Error loading configuration: {err}");
@@ -1921,29 +1914,6 @@ async fn load_bootstrap_config_or_exit(
     }
 }
 
-/// Point an unconfigured install at Z.ai rather than OpenAI.
-///
-/// The default model is served by Z.ai, but an unset `model_provider` resolves
-/// to OpenAI, so a fresh install opens on OpenAI's login screen and then talks
-/// to the wrong endpoint. Correcting the resolved provider here instead of in
-/// the config loader keeps the default out of the many in-process harnesses
-/// that build a `Config` and expect OpenAI's wire format.
-fn apply_default_model_provider(config: &mut Config) {
-    if config
-        .config_layer_stack
-        .effective_config()
-        .get("model_provider")
-        .is_some()
-    {
-        return;
-    }
-    let Some(provider) = config.model_providers.get(ZAI_PROVIDER_ID) else {
-        return;
-    };
-    config.model_provider = provider.clone();
-    config.model_provider_id = ZAI_PROVIDER_ID.to_string();
-}
